== Module (chicken syntax)

This module has support for syntax- and module handling. This module
is used by default, unless a program is compiled with the
{{-explicit-use}} option.

=== Macro transformers

Macro transformers are procedures you can use in a {{define-syntax}}
context to register a procedure that can transform s-expressions into
other s-expressions.  Only use these when you need to break hygiene in
a controlled way; for many use cases {{syntax-rules}} is more
appropriate, as it offers stronger guarantees of hygiene, is more
high-level and is standard R7RS Scheme.

For those situations where you need more control, however, CHICKEN
supports three kinds of low-level macros: so-called explicit renaming
and implicit renaming macros, and syntactic closures.

==== Explicit renaming macros

The low-level macro facility that CHICKEN provides is called "explicit
renaming" and allows writing hygienic or non-hygienic macros
procedurally.  When given the return value of one of the
procedures {{er-macro-transformer}} or {{ir-macro-transformer}}
instead of a {{syntax-rules}} form, {{define-syntax}} evaluates the
procedure in a distinct expansion environment (initially having access
to the exported identifiers of the {{scheme}} module). The procedure
takes an expression and two other arguments and returns a transformed
expression.

For example, the transformation procedure for a {{call}} macro such
that {{(call proc arg ...)}} expands into {{(proc arg ...)}} can be
written as

  (er-macro-transformer
    (lambda (exp rename compare)
      (cdr exp)))

Expressions are represented as lists in the traditional manner,
except that identifiers are represented as special uninterned symbols.

The second argument to a transformation procedure is a renaming procedure that
takes the representation of an identifier as its argument and returns the
representation of a fresh identifier that occurs nowhere else in the
program.  For example, the transformation procedure for a simplified
version of the {{let}} macro might be written as

  (er-macro-transformer
    (lambda (exp rename compare)
      (let ((vars (map car (cadr exp)))
            (inits (map cadr (cadr exp)))
            (body (cddr exp)))
        `((lambda ,vars ,@body)
          ,@inits))))

This would not be hygienic, however.  A hygienic {{let}} macro must
rename the identifier {{lambda}} to protect it from being captured by
a local binding.  The renaming effectively creates a fresh alias for
{{lambda}}, one that cannot be captured by any subsequent binding:

  (er-macro-transformer
    (lambda (exp rename compare)
      (let ((vars (map car (cadr exp)))
            (inits (map cadr (cadr exp)))
            (body (cddr exp)))
        `((,(rename 'lambda) ,vars ,@body)
          ,@inits))))

The expression returned by the transformation procedure will be
expanded in the syntactic environment obtained from the syntactic
environment of the macro application by binding any fresh identifiers
generated by the renaming procedure to the denotations of the original
identifiers in the syntactic environment in which the macro was
defined.  This means that a renamed identifier will denote the same
thing as the original identifier unless the transformation procedure
that renamed the identifier placed an occurrence of it in a binding
position.

Identifiers obtained from any two calls to the renaming procedure with
the same argument will necessarily be the same, but will denote the
same syntactical binding. It is an error if the renaming procedure is
called after the transformation procedure has returned.

The third argument to a transformation procedure is a comparison
predicate that takes the representations of two identifiers as its
arguments and returns true if and only if they denote the same thing
in the syntactic environment that will be used to expand the
transformed macro application.  For example, the transformation
procedure for a simplified version of the {{cond}} macro can be written
as

  (er-macro-transformer
    (lambda (exp rename compare)
      (let ((clauses (cdr exp)))
        (if (null? clauses)
            `(,(rename 'quote) unspecified)
            (let* ((first (car clauses))
                   (rest (cdr clauses))
                   (test (car first)))
              (cond ((and (symbol? test)
                          (compare test (rename 'else)))
                     `(,(rename 'begin) ,@(cdr first)))
                    (else `(,(rename 'if)
                            ,test
                             (,(rename 'begin) ,@(cdr first))
                             (,(rename 'cond) ,@rest)))))))))

In this example the identifier {{else}} is renamed before being passed
to the comparison predicate, so the comparison will be true if and
only if the test expression is an identifier that denotes the same
thing in the syntactic environment of the expression being transformed
as {{else}} denotes in the syntactic environment in which the {{cond}}
macro was defined.  If {{else}} were not renamed before being passed to
the comparison predicate, then it would match a local variable that
happened to be named {{else}}, and the macro would not be hygienic.
The final recursive call to {{cond}} also needs to be renamed because
someone might create an alias for this macro and use it in a {{let}}
where {{cond}} is an ordinary variable.

Some macros are non-hygienic by design.  For example, the
following defines a {{loop}} macro that implicitly binds {{exit}} to an
escape procedure.  The binding of {{exit}} is intended to capture free
references to {{exit}} in the body of the loop, so {{exit}} is not
renamed.

  (define-syntax loop
    (er-macro-transformer
      (lambda (x r c)
        (let ((body (cdr x)))
          `(,(r 'call-with-current-continuation)
            (,(r 'lambda) (exit)
             (,(r 'let) ,(r 'f) () ,@body (,(r 'f)))))))))

Suppose a {{while}} macro is implemented using {{loop}}, with the intent
that {{exit}} may be used to escape from the {{while}} loop.  The {{while}}
macro cannot be written as

  (define-syntax while
    (syntax-rules ()
      ((while test body ...)
       (loop (if (not test) (exit #f))
             body ...))))

because the reference to {{exit}} that is inserted by the {{while}} macro
is intended to be captured by the binding of {{exit}} that will be
inserted by the {{loop}} macro.  In other words, this {{while}} macro is
not hygienic.  Like {{loop}}, it must be written using procedurally:

  (define-syntax while
    (er-macro-transformer
      (lambda (x r c)
        (let ((test (cadr x))
              (body (cddr x)))
          `(,(r 'loop)
            (,(r 'if) (,(r 'not) ,test) (exit #f))
            ,@body)))))

Think about it: If we ''did'' rename {{exit}}, it would refer to an
{{exit}} procedure existing in the context of the macro's definition.
That one [[Unit library#exit|actually exists]]; it is the procedure
that exits the Scheme interpreter.  Definitely ''not'' the one we want :)
So now we make it refer to an {{exit}} that's locally bound in the
environment where the macro is expanded.

Note: this implementation of explicit-renaming macros allows passing
arbitrary expressions to the renaming and comparison procedures. When
being renamed, a fresh copy of the expression will be produced, with all
identifiers renamed appropriately. Comparison also supports arbitrary
expressions as arguments.


===== er-macro-transformer

<procedure>(er-macro-transformer TRANSFORMER)</procedure>

Returns an explicit-renaming macro transformer procedure created from
the procedural macro body {{TRANSFORMER}}, which is a procedure of
three arguments.

This procedure will be called on expansion with the complete
s-expression of the macro invocation, a rename procedure that
hygienically renames identifiers and a comparison procedure that
compares (possibly renamed) identifiers (see the section "Explicit
renaming macros" below for a detailed explanation on non-R7RS macros).

Implementation note: this procedure currently just returns its
argument unchanged and is available for writing low-level macros in a
more portable fashion, without hard-coding the signature of a
transformer procedure.

=== Implicit renaming macros

Explicit renaming macros generally require the user to perform quite a
few renames, because most identifiers that aren't taken from the input
expression should generally be inserted hygienically.  It would make
more sense to give the output expression as-is, and only explicitly
convert those identifiers that you want to treat as ''unhygienic''.

This can be done with implicit renaming macros.  They just swap the
default insertion "mode" from unhygienic to hygienic, so to speak.
Here's the {{cond}} example from the previous section as an ir-macro:


  (ir-macro-transformer
    (lambda (exp inject compare)
      (let ((clauses (cdr exp)))
        (if (null? clauses)
            `(quote unspecified)
            (let* ((first (car clauses))
                   (rest (cdr clauses))
                   (test (car first)))
              (cond ((and (symbol? test)
                          (compare test 'else))
                     `(begin ,@(cdr first)))
                    (else `(if ,test
                               (begin ,@(cdr first))
                               (cond ,@rest)))))))))

In this example the identifier {{else}} does ''not'' need to be renamed
before being passed to the comparison predicate because it is already
''implicitly'' renamed.  This comparison will also be true if and
only if the test expression is an identifier that denotes the same
thing in the syntactic environment of the expression being transformed
as {{else}} denotes in the syntactic environment in which the {{cond}}
macro was defined.  If {{else}} were not renamed before being passed to
the comparison predicate, then it would match a local variable that
happened to be named {{else}}, and the macro would not be hygienic.

As you can see, the code is a lot clearer because it isn't obscured
by excessive renaming.

Here's the {{loop}} macro so you can see how hygiene can be broken
with implicit renaming macros:

  (define-syntax loop
    (ir-macro-transformer
      (lambda (expr inject compare)
        (let ((body (cdr expr)))
          `(call-with-current-continuation
            (lambda (,(inject 'exit))
             (let f () ,@body (f))))))))

The {{while}} macro is a little trickier: do we inject the call to
{{exit}} or not?  Just like the explicit renaming macro version
did ''not'' rename it, we must inject it to allow it to be captured
by the {{loop}} macro:

  (define-syntax while
    (ir-macro-transformer
      (lambda (expr inject compare)
        (let ((test (cadr expr))
              (body (cddr expr)))
          `(loop
            (if (not ,test) (,(inject 'exit) #f))
            ,@body)))))

Note: Just like explicit renaming macros, this implementation of
implicit renaming macros allow passing arbitrary expressions to
the injection and comparison procedures.  The injection procedure
also return fresh copies of its input.


===== ir-macro-transformer

<procedure>(ir-macro-transformer TRANSFORMER)</procedure>

This procedure accepts a ''reverse'' syntax transformer, also known as
an ''implicit renaming macro transformer''.  This is a transformer which
works almost like er-macro-transformer, except the rename and compare
procedures it receives work a little differently.

The rename procedure is now called {{inject}} and instead of renaming
the identifier to be resolved in the macro's definition environment,
it will explicitly ''inject'' the identifier to be resolved in the
expansion environment.  Any non-injected identifiers in the output
expression produced by the transformer will be implicitly renamed to
refer to the macro's environment instead.  All identifiers in the
input expression are of course implicitly injected just like with
explicit renaming macros.  See the section above for a more complete
explanation.

To compare an input identifier you can generally compare to the bare
symbol and only free identifiers will match.  In practice, this means
that when you would call e.g.  {{(compare (cadr expression) (rename
'x))}} in an ER macro, you simply call {{(compare (cadr expression)
'x)}} in the IR macro.  Likewise, an ''unhygienic'' ER macro's
comparison {{(compare sym 'abc)}} should be written as {{(compare sym
(inject 'abc))}} in an IR macro.


=== Syntactic closures

Syntactic closures (Alan Bawden and Jonathan Rees, ''Syntactic
Closures'', 1988 ACM Conference on Lisp and Functional Programming)
are a third kind of low-level macro, compatible with MIT Scheme,
chibi-scheme and the old {{syntactic-closures}} egg.  The paper is the
reference for their semantics; this section quotes its examples.

A ''syntactic closure'' is a form together with the ''syntactic
environment'' in which its names must be interpreted, and a list of
''free names'' that are instead interpreted where the closure is
placed.  As the paper puts it, "a syntactic closure carries its own
context with it".  A macro is written as a procedure, an ''expander'',
that receives a syntactic environment instead of rename and compare
procedures, and makes hygiene explicit by closing forms in the
environment where they belong:

* the parts of the macro form are closed in the ''usage'' environment,
where the macro is used;
* the code the macro adds is closed in the ''macro'' environment, where
it was defined, or in an environment it knows, such as
{{scheme-syntactic-environment}}.

The paper's first examples are {{push}}, {{or}} and {{catch}}.  With
{{sc-macro-transformer}}, whose result is closed in the macro
environment, they read:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax push
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (exp env)
     (let ((obj-exp (make-syntactic-closure env '() (cadr exp)))
           (list-var (make-syntactic-closure env '() (caddr exp))))
       `(set! ,list-var (cons ,obj-exp ,list-var))))))

(define-syntax or2
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (exp env)
     (let ((exp-1 (make-syntactic-closure env '() (cadr exp)))
           (exp-2 (make-syntactic-closure env '() (caddr exp))))
       `((lambda (temp) (if temp temp ,exp-2)) ,exp-1)))))

(define-syntax catch
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (exp env)
     (let ((body-exp (make-syntactic-closure env '(throw) (cadr exp))))
       `(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (throw) ,body-exp))))))

(let ((stack '()))
  (let ((cons 6)) (push 'foo stack))          ; cons is not captured
  stack)                                      ; => (foo)
(let ((temp 'tv)) (or2 #f temp))              ; => tv, temp is the user's
(catch (+ 5 (throw 'x)))                      ; => x, throw is captured
</enscript>

{{set!}}, {{cons}}, {{lambda}} and {{if}} are interpreted where the
macros were defined, so local bindings of these names at the call site
do not affect them; the user's {{temp}} is closed in the usage
environment, so the macro's {{temp}} cannot capture it; and the body of
{{catch}} leaves {{throw}} free, so the {{throw}} bound by the macro
captures it.

==== How syntactic closures work in CHICKEN

Syntactic closures are implemented on top of the renaming expander:
when an sc or rsc transformer returns, every syntactic closure in its
result is replaced by an equivalent renamed form ("lowered").  Hence
sc, rsc, er, ir and {{syntax-rules}} macros can be freely mixed: they
may expand into each other and receive each other's output.  All of
the procedures below are available at expansion time without an
explicit import, like {{er-macro-transformer}}.

As in the paper's implementation, every syntactic closure is an
identifier context of its own: a name in a closure means what it means
in the closure's environment, and nothing that the surrounding output
binds can capture it.  Two closures of the same name are two
different identifiers: a closure used as a binding name binds only
that closure.  To bind a name given by the caller in a body supplied by
the caller, do as the paper's {{let}} does: put the name itself in the
binding position and leave it free in the closure of the body.  A
closure object can also be used both as the binder and the reference:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax bind-1
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (exp env)
     `(let ((,(cadr exp) 1))
        ,(make-syntactic-closure env (list (cadr exp)) (caddr exp))))))
(bind-1 x (+ x 1))                                      ; => 2

(define-syntax twice-bound
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (exp env)
     (let ((v (close-syntax (cadr exp) env)))          ; one closure
       `(let ((,v 21)) (* 2 ,v))))))
(twice-bound x)                                         ; => 42
</enscript>

Writing {{(let ((,(close-syntax 'x env) 1)) ,(close-syntax 'x env))}}
instead refers to the {{x}} of the usage environment, not to the
binding, as in MIT Scheme and chibi-scheme.  This also applies to
named {{let}}, {{receive}}, {{do}} and the like.

Only the free names of a closure take their meaning from the place
where it is put, and this also holds when the closure is passed to
another macro.  In the paper a macro used inside a closure has that
closure's environment as its usage environment: in

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax aif
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (form env)
     `(let ((it ,(close-syntax (cadr form) env)))
        (if it
            ,(make-syntactic-closure env '(it) (caddr form))
            ,(close-syntax (cadddr form) env))))))

(aif (memq 'b '(a b c)) (aif (cadr it) (list it) 'no) 'no)  ; => (c)
(catch (list 'outer (catch (throw 'inner))))              ; => (outer inner)
(catch (list 'outer (or2 #f (catch (throw 'inner)))))     ; => (outer inner)
</enscript>

the inner {{it}} and {{throw}} are those of the inner macro, also when
a macro that closes its operands in its usage environment, such as
{{or2}}, stands between the two, or when the inner call is built by a
{{syntax-rules}}, er or ir macro, e.g.
{{(define-syntax my-catch (syntax-rules () ((_ e) (catch e))))}} used
as {{(catch (list 'outer (my-catch (throw 'inner))))}}.  A closure
handed to a macro is not free text of that macro's form, so the free
names of the macro's own closures do not capture it:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax pass
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (form env)
     `(aif ,(close-syntax (cadr form) env) ,(close-syntax (caddr form) env) #f))))

(let ((it 'user)) (pass 1 it))                            ; => user
</enscript>

Conversely, what a macro called inside a closure binds in its usage
environment captures the free names of that closure as the user wrote
them in the macro form, as in the paper, also when the macro is an rsc
macro that writes the binder as a plain symbol:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax rcatch
  (rsc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (form env)
     `(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (throw) ,(cadr form))))))

(catch (list 'outer (rcatch (throw 'inner))))             ; => (outer inner)
</enscript>

A name of the usage environment is written in the expansion as it is
(and not as a fresh alias) when that cannot change what it refers to,
that is, when the expansion contains no other identifier of that name.
So the output of a macro looks like the code the user wrote, and
non-hygienic er, ir and rsc macros used in it see the user's names.

The paper leaves definitions unspecified (its section 6).  In CHICKEN a
body that defines a name, either directly or through a closure of that
name made in the usage environment of a macro used in the body,
defines the name itself, and every closure of the name in that
environment refers to the definition.  (A macro is used in the body
when it is expanded while the body's definitions are collected,
directly or through other macros.)  Closures made in an environment
outside the body, for instance by a macro whose output contains the
body or kept by an expander from an earlier expansion, keep their
meaning, as they would with {{letrec*}}.  So a macro can write
{{(define ,(close-syntax name env) ...)}} and
{{(set! ,(close-syntax name env) ...)}}, with two different closures,
and both refer to the user's {{name}}, in a body as at toplevel.  A
binder of the name inside the body that is not one of those closures,
such as the parameter of a {{(lambda (x) ...)}} made by another
closure of the macro, is renamed apart where needed, so that it does
not capture them (see the differences below for the binding forms this
works with).

A syntactic environment can be used to make closures (and to compare
identifiers) while the expansion it belongs to runs, and later in the
expansion of code within its scope; for instance by an expander bound
with {{extend-syntactic-environment}}, as the paper's {{with-macro}}
does.  (The usage and macro environments of a toplevel expansion stay
usable in later toplevel code, which is within their scope.)  The
environment that a capture placed inside the output, or at the top of
a closure with free names, receives stands for bindings of that output,
so it can only be used while its expansion runs.  Using an environment
anywhere else signals an error.  Closures that were made
while their environment could be used keep their meaning, and can be
placed in the output of later expansions.

==== Differences from the paper and from MIT Scheme

* Syntactic closures must occur in the result of an sc or rsc
transformer, an expander, or a procedure given to
{{capture-syntactic-environment}}.  An er or ir transformer cannot
return them, except inside quoted data.
* Inside {{quote}} (and vector literals) closures are replaced by their
forms; the paper's implementation leaves the closure objects there.
* Unclosed output of an expander is interpreted in the macro
environment, as the output of {{sc-macro-transformer}} is; the paper's
implementation signals an error.
* Variables shadow keywords, and a closed keyword is a keyword, as in
R7RS; the paper's implementation dispatches on the raw symbol at the
head of a form.
* {{capture-syntactic-environment}} (MIT Scheme's extension) calls its
procedure once, when the output of the transformer is lowered (see
below).
* A binder in the result of a capture placed inside the output does not
capture closures made in the captured environment, and a binder of a
name inside a body does not capture the closures that define the name
(see above), only where the binding form is one of the standard
{{lambda}}, {{let}} (also named {{let}}), {{let*}}, {{letrec}},
{{letrec*}}, {{do}} or, in the bodies of these, {{define}}.  A binder
made by another macro there, e.g. by {{receive}} or a user's macro, can
still capture such a closure.
* Quoted data in the output of an sc or rsc transformer that is too
large or too deep to walk (more than 4000000 pairs, or nested more
than 1000 deep), in particular circular data, is stripped rather than
lowered: closures in it are replaced by their forms and lose their
identity (which only matters for templates of {{with-macro}}), and
captures in it are not called.
* Definitions, as described above.
* A macro call that an er, ir or {{syntax-rules}} macro builds gets
the context of the form that macro was called with (see the nested
{{catch}} above).  CHICKEN's own macros ({{let}}, {{cond}},
{{cut}} etc.) are taken to pass the forms they receive on as they are;
a call of an sc macro that one of them builds anew, e.g. by
{{(cut aif <> it #f)}}, does not see the code around it as its usage
environment.
* Input forms may contain identifiers renamed by other macros.  Compare
identifiers with {{identifier=?}}, or use {{identifier->symbol}}, and
never use {{eq?}} or {{case}} on them.
* A user name written as it is (see above) can be captured by a
non-hygienic er, ir or rsc macro that the expansion calls, like any
user code.  When an expansion uses a name of its usage environment
that also occurs as it is in its own macro form, the name is taken to
be that occurrence.
* The code of a transformer that is itself part of lowered output (a
{{let-syntax}}, {{letrec-syntax}} or {{define-syntax}} in the output
of an sc macro, or written by the user inside a closure with free
names, such as the body of {{aif}}) loses the renaming of the symbols
it quotes, as all quoted data does.  So {{'it}} returned by an sc
transformer there, and {{(r 'it)}} or {{(i 'it)}} in an er or ir one,
mean what the plain name {{it}} means where the local macro is defined
(here the global {{it}}), not the binding that the enclosing output
introduced; in the paper they keep their meaning.  {{syntax-rules}}
templates, a closure of the name in the transformer's usage
environment ({{(close-syntax 'it env)}}) and the {{with-macro}} of
{{scheme-macrology}} are not affected.  Likewise a local macro defined
in the output of an sc macro cannot refer, by a quoted name, to a
variable private to that output.
* An exported macro that expands into references to bindings private to
its module must list them as indirect exports, as with
{{er-macro-transformer}}.
* The procedures are exported by {{(chicken syntax)}}, which every
toplevel imports by default, rather than by a separate library.  A
definition of one of their names (e.g. a helper {{identifier?}}) at
toplevel, or in a module that imports {{(chicken syntax)}} whole,
assigns the binding of {{(chicken syntax)}} for the whole program (the
compiler warns about it), as for any imported binding.  Import with
{{except}} to keep such a definition private:
{{(import (except (chicken syntax) identifier?))}}.  The expander and
{{scheme-macrology}} do not use the exported bindings.

==== sc-macro-transformer

<procedure>(sc-macro-transformer TRANSFORMER)</procedure>

Returns a syntactic-closure macro transformer.  {{TRANSFORMER}} is
called with the complete macro form and the ''usage'' environment.
Its result is interpreted in the ''macro'' environment: plain symbols
in it refer to the bindings visible where the macro was defined, and
binders it introduces are fresh.  Parts of the input form must be
closed in the usage environment (with {{make-syntactic-closure}} or
{{close-syntax}}) to keep their meaning.  See {{push}}, {{or2}} and
{{catch}} above.

==== rsc-macro-transformer

<procedure>(rsc-macro-transformer TRANSFORMER)</procedure>

The reverse: {{TRANSFORMER}} is called with the macro form and the
''macro'' environment, and its result is interpreted in the usage
environment.  Input forms can therefore be used as they are, while the
identifiers the macro introduces must be closed in the macro
environment to be hygienic:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax swap!
  (rsc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (form env)
     (let ((a (cadr form)) (b (caddr form))
           (tmp (close-syntax 'tmp env)))
       `(,(close-syntax 'let env) ((,tmp ,a))
         (,(close-syntax 'set! env) ,a ,b)
         (,(close-syntax 'set! env) ,b ,tmp))))))
</enscript>

If either kind of transformer returns its input form unchanged
({{eq?}}), the form is not lowered.  In {{define-compiler-syntax}}
this declines the optimization (an sc compiler-syntax transformer can
also decline by returning {{(close-syntax form env)}}); in an ordinary
macro it is reported as an endless expansion, as for er and ir
transformers.

==== make-syntactic-closure

<procedure>(make-syntactic-closure ENV FREE-NAMES FORM)</procedure>

Returns a syntactic closure of {{FORM}} in the syntactic environment
{{ENV}}.  Names in {{FORM}} are interpreted in {{ENV}}, except those in
the list {{FREE-NAMES}}, which take the meaning they have where the
closure is placed (for example, a binding introduced around it by the
macro's output, as {{throw}} in {{catch}} above).  If {{FORM}} is a
constant other than a vector (a number, string, character, boolean
and so on) it is returned unchanged; a vector may contain names, and
is closed.  A member of {{FREE-NAMES}} is returned unchanged too,
except a name that the expansion received in a closure nested in its
macro form (see {{pass}} above), which is closed.

Closures may appear anywhere in a macro's output: as expressions,
operators, {{set!}} targets, binding or definition names, inside
{{quote}} (where they are removed) and as arguments to other macros.
A closure may be closed again; the inner closure keeps its own
environment.

==== close-syntax

<procedure>(close-syntax FORM ENV)</procedure>

Equivalent to {{(make-syntactic-closure ENV '() FORM)}}.

==== make-syntactic-closure-list

<procedure>(make-syntactic-closure-list ENV FREE-NAMES FORMS)</procedure>

Closes each element of the proper list {{FORMS}} with
{{make-syntactic-closure}}.  {{ENV}} and {{FREE-NAMES}} are checked
also when {{FORMS}} is empty.

==== capture-syntactic-environment

<procedure>(capture-syntactic-environment PROC)</procedure>

Returns a form which, when it occurs in the output of a transformer,
is replaced by the result of calling {{PROC}} with the syntactic
environment of that place.  When the capture is the whole output, that
is the macro environment of an sc transformer, the usage environment of
an rsc transformer, or the environment of a closure whose whole form it
is.  The typical use is obtaining the macro environment in an sc
transformer:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax my-cond
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (form env)
     (capture-syntactic-environment
      (lambda (menv)
        (let loop ((clauses (cdr form)))
          (cond ((null? clauses) '(if #f #f))
                ((identifier=? env (caar clauses) menv 'else)
                 `(begin ,@(make-syntactic-closure-list env '() (cdar clauses))))
                (else
                 `(if ,(close-syntax (caar clauses) env)
                      (begin ,@(make-syntactic-closure-list env '() (cdar clauses)))
                      ,(loop (cdr clauses)))))))))))
</enscript>

A capture placed inside the output gets the environment at that place,
so closures made in it see the bindings the output makes around it, as
in this example from the MIT Scheme manual:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax loop-until
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (exp env)
     (let ((id (cadr exp))
           (init (caddr exp))
           (test (cadddr exp))
           (return (cadddr (cdr exp)))
           (step (cadddr (cddr exp)))
           (close (lambda (exp free) (make-syntactic-closure env free exp))))
       `(letrec ((loop
                  ,(capture-syntactic-environment
                    (lambda (env)
                      `(lambda (,id)
                         (,(make-syntactic-closure env '() `if)
                          ,(close test (list id))
                          ,(close return (list id))
                          (,(make-syntactic-closure env '() `loop)
                           ,(close step (list id)))))))))
          (loop ,(close init '())))))))

(loop-until i 0 (> i 5) (* i 10) (+ i 1))               ; => 60
</enscript>

A binder in the capture's own result does not capture closures made in
the environment: there {{(make-syntactic-closure env '() 'loop)}}
refers to the {{letrec}} binding also when the user's {{id}} is
{{loop}} (or {{if}}), as in MIT Scheme:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(loop-until loop 0 (> loop 5) (* loop 10) (+ loop 1))   ; => 60
</enscript>

Unlike MIT Scheme, {{PROC}} is called once, when the transformer's
output is lowered and before that output is expanded, so
{{identifier=?}} in the environment it receives does not see the
bindings of the output around the capture.

==== expander-macro-transformer

<procedure>(expander-macro-transformer EXPANDER)</procedure>

Returns a macro transformer for an ''expander'' in the sense of the
paper: a procedure {{(lambda (syntactic-env exp) ...)}} that is called
with the usage environment and the macro form (note the order of the
arguments) and returns a syntactic closure, usually one closed in
{{scheme-syntactic-environment}}.  The expanders of the paper (its
section 3.2) can be used as they are:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define (push-expander syntactic-env exp)
  (let ((obj-exp (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (cadr exp)))
        (list-var (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (caddr exp))))
    (make-syntactic-closure scheme-syntactic-environment '()
      `(set! ,list-var (cons ,obj-exp ,list-var)))))

(define (or-expander syntactic-env exp)
  (let ((exp-1 (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (cadr exp)))
        (exp-2 (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (caddr exp))))
    (make-syntactic-closure scheme-syntactic-environment '()
      `((lambda (temp) (if temp temp ,exp-2)) ,exp-1))))

(define (catch-expander syntactic-env exp)
  (let ((body-exp (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '(throw) (cadr exp))))
    (make-syntactic-closure scheme-syntactic-environment '()
      `(call-with-current-continuation (lambda (throw) ,body-exp)))))

(define-syntax push (expander-macro-transformer push-expander))
(define-syntax or2 (expander-macro-transformer or-expander))
(define-syntax catch (expander-macro-transformer catch-expander))
</enscript>

(In compiled code, define the expanders with {{define-for-syntax}} or
inside {{begin-for-syntax}}.)  An unclosed result is interpreted in the
macro environment, as with {{sc-macro-transformer}}.

==== scheme-syntactic-environment

<constant>scheme-syntactic-environment</constant>

The ''advertised'' environment of the paper (section 4.2): the names
of the {{scheme}} module have their standard meaning, whatever local or
module bindings of the same names are visible where the closure is
used.  Its variables are absolute references (e.g. to
{{scheme#cons}}), and its syntactic keywords are the standard {{if}},
{{lambda}}, {{let}}, {{set!}} and so on.  So in {{push-expander}}
above, "the mapping of the identifier named cons found in
scheme-syntactic-environment will be the global variable named cons,
rather than any local variables that happen to have the same name".
Other names mean what they mean in the macro environment of the
expansion that made the closure: for a toplevel macro, the toplevel;
for a macro defined in a module, the module.

==== core-syntactic-environment

<constant>core-syntactic-environment</constant>

The paper's core environment: only the primitive keywords {{quote}},
{{if}}, {{begin}}, {{set!}} and {{lambda}} are keywords; the other names
are variables, those of the {{scheme}} module with their standard
meaning.  Using another keyword of the macro environment in it signals
a syntax error.  The derived syntax is built over it with
{{scheme-macrology}}.

==== extend-syntactic-environment

<procedure>(extend-syntactic-environment ENV KEYWORD EXPANDER)</procedure>

Returns a new syntactic environment that is like {{ENV}} except that
the symbol {{KEYWORD}} is a macro expanded by {{EXPANDER}}: either an
expander procedure (see {{expander-macro-transformer}}) or a
transformer made by {{sc-macro-transformer}},
{{rsc-macro-transformer}}, {{er-macro-transformer}},
{{ir-macro-transformer}} or {{syntax-rules}}.  The macro environment of
a transformer is that of the expansion that calls
{{extend-syntactic-environment}}.  The paper's example (section 4.1)

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(extend-syntactic-environment scheme-syntactic-environment 'push push-expander)
</enscript>

"returns a new syntactic environment in which expressions of the form
{{(push ...)}} are expanded by the expander push-expander.  Any other
expression is interpreted according to scheme-syntactic-environment."
Forms closed in the new environment can use the keyword; it is not
visible anywhere else.  An inner extension shadows an outer one with
the same keyword, also when both are made by nested uses of one macro:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-syntax with-k
  (sc-macro-transformer
   (lambda (form env)
     (make-syntactic-closure
      (extend-syntactic-environment env 'k
        (lambda (e x)
          (make-syntactic-closure scheme-syntactic-environment '()
            (list 'quote (cadr form)))))
      '() `(begin ,@(cddr form))))))

(with-k a (list (k) (with-k b (k)) (k)))         ; => (a b a)
</enscript>

Section 1 of the paper uses such an environment for {{contorted}},
whose auxiliary keyword {{use}} never reaches the caller's subforms:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-for-syntax (contorted-expander syntactic-env exp)
  (let* ((test (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (cadr exp)))
         (x (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (caddr exp)))
         (y (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (cadddr exp)))
         (use-env
          (extend-syntactic-environment
           scheme-syntactic-environment 'use
           (lambda (use-syntactic-env use-exp)
             (make-syntactic-closure scheme-syntactic-environment '()
               `(,(cadr use-exp) ,x ,y))))))
    (make-syntactic-closure scheme-syntactic-environment '()
      `(if ,test
           ,(make-syntactic-closure use-env '() '(use and))
           ,(make-syntactic-closure use-env '() '(use or))))))

(define-syntax contorted (expander-macro-transformer contorted-expander))

(let ((use (lambda (a b) 'user-use)))
  (contorted #f (use 1 2) 3))                     ; => user-use
</enscript>

A ''macrology'' (paper section 4.3) is a procedure from syntactic
environments to syntactic environments that adds a collection of
related keywords:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-for-syntax (pop-expander syntactic-env exp)
  (let ((var (make-syntactic-closure syntactic-env '() (cadr exp))))
    (make-syntactic-closure scheme-syntactic-environment '()
      `(let ((top (car ,var))) (set! ,var (cdr ,var)) top))))

(define-for-syntax (stack-macrology env)
  (extend-syntactic-environment
   (extend-syntactic-environment env 'push push-expander)
   'pop pop-expander))

(define-syntax with-stack-ops      ; push and pop only in its body
  (expander-macro-transformer
   (lambda (env exp)
     (make-syntactic-closure (stack-macrology env) '()
       `(begin ,@(cdr exp))))))
</enscript>

==== scheme-macrology

<procedure>(scheme-macrology SYNTACTIC-ENV)</procedure>

The paper's Scheme macrology (section 4.3 and appendix): returns an
environment that extends {{SYNTACTIC-ENV}}, which must give meaning to
the primitive keywords, with the derived keywords {{delay}}, {{or}},
{{and}}, {{let}}, {{cond}}, {{case}}, {{with-macro}} and
{{with-macro-rec}}, all defined in terms of the primitive keywords of
{{SYNTACTIC-ENV}} and of each other.  The paper's example obtains an
environment in which the derived keywords use an alternative {{if}}:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-for-syntax (new-if-expander syntactic-env exp)   ; negates the test
  (let ((parts (make-syntactic-closure-list syntactic-env '() (cdr exp))))
    (make-syntactic-closure core-syntactic-environment '()
      `(if (not ,(car parts)) ,@(cdr parts)))))

(define-for-syntax new-if-syntactic-environment
  (scheme-macrology
   (extend-syntactic-environment core-syntactic-environment 'if new-if-expander)))

(define-syntax with-new-if
  (expander-macro-transformer
   (lambda (syntactic-env exp)
     (make-syntactic-closure new-if-syntactic-environment '() (cadr exp)))))

(with-new-if (or #f 5))                            ; => #f
</enscript>

{{(with-macro (KEYWORD . PARAMETERS) TEMPLATE BODY ...)}} (paper
section 4.4) binds {{KEYWORD}} in {{BODY}} to a macro whose replacement
is {{TEMPLATE}}, evaluated as standard Scheme at expansion time with
the {{PARAMETERS}} bound to closures of the arguments; the replacement
is closed in the environment of the {{with-macro}} form, so

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(let ((adjoin cons))
  (with-macro (push frob stack) `(set! ,stack (adjoin ,frob ,stack))
    (let ((adjoin +))
      (push (adjoin n m) sum-stack))))
</enscript>

behaves as {{(set! sum-stack (cons (+ n m) sum-stack))}}.  The
symbols the template puts into the replacement keep the identity they
have in the {{with-macro}} form; two different identifiers of the same
name in quoted data of the template (e.g. a closure that an expander
quoted into it) stay distinct.
{{with-macro-rec}} closes the replacement inside its body instead, so
the macro can be recursive:

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(with-macro-rec (or exp . other-exps)
    (if (null? other-exps)
        exp
        `(let ((temp ,exp)) (if temp temp (or ,@other-exps))))
  (or #f #f 7))                                    ; => 7
</enscript>

These forms are available in code closed in an environment made by
{{scheme-macrology}}, e.g. {{(scheme-macrology core-syntactic-environment)}}.

==== identifier?

<procedure>(identifier? X)</procedure>

Returns {{#t}} if {{X}} is a symbol (including the renamed symbols
produced by other macros) or a syntactic closure of an identifier.

==== identifier=?

<procedure>(identifier=? ENV1 ID1 ENV2 ID2)</procedure>

Returns {{#t}} if {{ID1}} interpreted in {{ENV1}} and {{ID2}}
interpreted in {{ENV2}} denote the same binding: the same local
variable, the same macro or special form, or the same global name.
Returns {{#f}} if either is not an identifier.

==== identifier->symbol

<procedure>(identifier->symbol ID)</procedure>

Returns the symbol {{ID}} was written as, removing syntactic closures
and renaming (see {{strip-syntax}}).

==== syntactic-closure?

<procedure>(syntactic-closure? X)</procedure>

==== syntactic-environment?

<procedure>(syntactic-environment? X)</procedure>

Type predicates.

==== strip-syntactic-closures

<procedure>(strip-syntactic-closures X)</procedure>

Returns a copy of {{X}} with all syntactic closures replaced by their
forms and renamed identifiers by their original names.  This is the
same as {{strip-syntax}}, which also removes syntactic closures.

=== Expanding macros

==== expand

<procedure>(expand X)</procedure>

If {{X}} is a macro-form, expand the macro (and repeat expansion
until expression is a non-macro form).  Returns the resulting expression.

==== expand1

<procedure>(expand1 X)</procedure>

If {{X}} is a macro-form, expand the macro only once.  Unlike
{{expand}}, does not repeat expansion when the resulting expression is
itself a macro-form.  Returns the resulting expression.

=== Macro helper procedures

==== begin-for-syntax

<macro>(begin-for-syntax EXP ...)</macro>

Equivalent to {{(begin EXP ...)}}, but performs the evaluation of the
expression during macro-expansion time, using the macro environment
rather than the interaction environment.

You can use this to define your own helper procedures that you can
call from a syntax transformer.

==== define-for-syntax

<macro>(define-for-syntax (NAME VAR ...) EXP1 ...)</macro><br>
<macro>(define-for-syntax (NAME VAR1 ... VARn . VARn+1) EXP1 ...)</macro><br>
<macro>(define-for-syntax NAME [VALUE])</macro>

Defines the toplevel variable {{NAME}} at macro-expansion time. This
can be helpful when you want to define support procedures for use in
macro-transformers, for example.

Essentially, this is a shorthand for
{{(begin-for-syntax (define ...))}}.

Note that {{define-for-syntax}} definitions within a module are
implicitly added to that module's import library. Refer to the
documentation on [[Modules#import-libraries|import libraries]]
for more information.


==== syntax

<procedure>(syntax EXPRESSION)</procedure>

This will quote the {{EXPRESSION}} for use in a syntax expansion.  Any
syntactic information will be stripped from the {{EXPRESSION}}.


==== strip-syntax

<procedure>(strip-syntax EXPRESSION)</procedure>

Strips all syntactical information from {{EXPRESSION}}, returning a
new expression where symbols have all context-information removed.

You should use this procedure whenever you want to manually construct
new identifiers, which an unhygienic macro can insert.  In some cases
it does not ''appear'' to be necessary to strip context information
when you use the macro, but you still should do it.  Sometimes
identifiers will not have been renamed (most often at toplevel), but
there may be other contexts in which identifiers ''will'' have been
renamed.

==== read-with-source-info

<procedure>(read-with-source-info [port filename])</procedure>

Exactly like {{read}} from the {{scheme}} module, except it
registers the expression it read into the line number database, so
that if {{(read-with-source-info)}} returns {{OBJ}},
{{(get-line-number OBJ)}} will return the line number in {{port}}.

The port argument may be omitted, in which case it defaults to the
value returned by {{current-input-port}}. It is an error to read from
a closed port. If {{filename}} is given, it designates the name
of the file associated. In case this argument is not given, the
currently opened source file is used. If no current source file
is opened, then the name defaults to "<stdin>".

==== get-line-number

<procedure>(get-line-number EXPR)</procedure>

If {{EXPR}} is a pair with the car being a symbol, and line-number
information is available for this expression, then this procedure
returns the associated source file and line number as a string. If
line-number information is not available, then {{#f}} is returned.

=== Compiler macros

==== define-compiler-syntax

<macro>(define-compiler-syntax NAME)</macro><br>
<macro>(define-compiler-syntax NAME TRANSFORMER)</macro><br>

Defines what is usually called a ''compiler macro'' in Lisp: {{NAME}}
should be the name of a globally or locally bound procedure. Any
direct call to this procedure will be transformed before compilation,
which allows arbitrary rewritings of function calls.

{{TRANSFORMER}} can be a {{syntax-rules}} expression or a transformer
procedure (as returned by {{er-macro-transformer}} or
{{ir-macro-transformer}}). Returning the original form in an
explicit/implicit-renaming macro or simply "falling trough" all
patterns in a {{syntax-rules}} form will keep the original expression
and compile it normally.

In the interpreter this form does nothing and returns an unspecified
value.

Compiler-syntax is always local to the current compilation unit and
can not be exported. Compiler-syntax defined inside a module is not
visible outside of that module.

{{define-compiler-syntax}} should only be used at top-level. Local
compiler-syntax can be defined with {{let-compiler-syntax}}.

<enscript highlight=scheme>
(define-compiler-syntax +
  (syntax-rules ()
    ((_) 1)
    ((_ x 0) x) ) )
</enscript>

If no transformer is given, then {{(define-compiler-syntax NAME)}}
removes any compiler-syntax definitions for {{NAME}}.


==== let-compiler-syntax

<macro>(let-compiler-syntax ((NAME [TRANSFORMER]) ...) BODY ...)</macro>

Allows definition local compiler macros, which are only applicable
inside {{BODY ...}}.  By not providing a {{TRANSFORMER}} expression,
compiler-syntax for specific identifiers can be temporarily disabled.

